    <div class="article-body">
      <p class="lead">Navigating a fast-food parlor when living with celiac disease, severe peanut anaphylaxis, or lactose intolerance can be an intimidating prospect. Behind the service counter at Dairy Queen, dozens of high-risk ingredients move at lightning speed: pulverized Oreo dust airborne near the spindle, malted milk powder canisters, warm peanut butter ladles, and shared hot fryers. However, contrary to common belief, Dairy Queen is far from off-limits. With strict protocol adherence and an understanding of kitchen line mechanics, sensitive diners can indulge with confidence. Below is our <strong>2026 Clinical &amp; Kitchen Cross-Contact Field Guide</strong>.</p>

      <h2>Anatomy of Cross-Contact: The 3 Kitchen Vectors</h2>
      <p>Before reviewing menu items, diners with medical sensitivities must understand how allergens migrate across prep stations:</p>

      <div style="background:#FAF7F2;border:1px solid #EDE4D6;border-radius:18px;padding:24px;margin:24px 0;">
        <h3 style="margin-top:0;color:#0B1E38;font-size:1.2rem;">The 3 High-Risk Contamination Points</h3>
        <ul style="margin:12px 0 0 0;padding-left:0;list-style:none;">
          <li style="padding:10px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #EBE1D2;">
            <strong>1. The High-Speed Spindle Splash-Collar:</strong> 
            When blending a Blizzard, the spinning blade operates at several thousand RPMs. Micro-shards of gluten-containing cookies and peanuts coat the protective rubber collar. If the machine is simply wiped with a moist cloth between treats, residual gluten transfers directly into your cup.
          </li>
          <li style="padding:10px 0;border-bottom:1px solid #EBE1D2;">
            <strong>2. Shared Commercial Fryer Oil:</strong> 
            While raw cut potatoes are naturally gluten-free, 95% of Dairy Queen locations share fry vats between French fries, breaded chicken tenders, Wisconsin cheese curds, and onion rings. The heated frying oil acts as a gluten suspension fluid, making fries unsafe for celiac diners.
          </li>
          <li style="padding:10px 0;">
            <strong>3. Hardened Cone Dip Wells:</strong> 
            When soft serve cones are inverted into warm chocolate or butterscotch dip vats, tiny crumbs of sugar cone often break off and remain submerged in the warm liquid.
          </li>
        </ul>
      </div>

